        ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
        ":question/id": "35ea5fbe-0a0e-53a6-8872-3cafe1bfb9c7",
        ":question/math-academy-id": "q-210859",
        ":question/problem": "Consider the following system of equations. \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}3s+4t=8 \\\\ s+5t=10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDescribe the steps needed to eliminate the variable $s$ from the system:\n\nTo eliminate $s,$ multiply the \n{{field-1}} \nequation by \n{{field-2}}, \nand add the equations.",
        ":question/worked-solution": "Notice that the coefficient of $s$ in the first equation is $3,$ while in the second equation it is $1.$ \n\nIf we multiply the second equation by $-3,$ then the coefficient of $s$ will turn into $-3,$ which will cancel with the coefficient of $3$ in the first equation.\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}3s+4t=8 \\\\ (\\,s+5t=10\\,)\\,\\times (-3)\\end{aligned}\\,\u27f9\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}3s+4t=8 \\\\ -3s-15t=-30\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when we add the two equations, the variable $s$ will be eliminated:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\cancel{3s}+4t & =8 \\\\ -\\cancel{3s}-15t & =-30 \\\\ (4t-15t) & =(8-30) \\\\ -11t & =-22 \\\\ t & =2\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is the following:\n\n*Multiply the $\\text{second}$ equation by $-3,$ and add the equations*."
      }
